## Branching of the task graph

If you need branching, you have to model tasks as parallel. See OPtimistic time stepping for Euler.
And then you add guards that ensure that always only one branch works.
SO we have to work with masking, as real branching is not supported by the task graph.


Why exclusivity doesn't fix the abort

Peano decides per sweep, not per cell, whether a sweep loads and stores a data object. The "Update cell" sweep declares QWithHalo as READ_AND_DISCARD. So it takes the patch off the stack for every cell and doesn't put it back, even for cells where UpdateCell doesn't fire. The run proves this. It aborts in time step 0, where every cell takes the traditional branch and UpdateCellShell fires nowhere. Still, the shell sweep tries to load QWithHalo and finds nothing.

Fix

At the task level your declarations are right: both siblings read the halo patch and are done with it. With the sequential compiler the two become consecutive sweeps, so only the second may discard. I'd let propagate_use_statements() handle this. Whenever a step declares READ_AND_DISCARD and a later step reads the same data before anything writes it, the compiler would upgrade the discard to READ_AND_WRITE. That would keep your declarations, work for any compiler that puts sibling tasks one after the other, and fit next to the pass I just added. Shall I implement that?


So we need a read-write. If these are smart pointers, it should not harm.


